build/
# Any CMake build tree at the root, not only the one called "build". A review build named
# build_genotype_review was swept into a commit by `git add -A` -- 448 generated files, 75 MB --
# and one directory name was all that stood between the rule and the repository.
/build*/
.DS_Store

*.o
*.obj
*.a
*.so
*.dylib
__pycache__/
*.claude/*
*.claude


#tests/real_data/gencode*
#tests/real_data/lpa*
#tests/real_data/gstm1*
#tests/real_data/cypd26*
tests/results

!tests/results/.gitkeep


# Local regenerated results (scripts/regen_results.sh)
results/
tests/real_data/*.gtf.gz
chiara_test/*


# Local-only working material. None of this belongs in the published repository:
#   experiments/  proof-of-concepts and the research record for the module that is not released
#   results/      regenerated output tables (scripts/regen_results.sh), ignored above
#   docs/reports/ dated review write-ups, kept for the authors rather than for users
#   docs/review_followups.md  the release ledger -- internal working state, not documentation
# An earlier rule tracked experiments/*.md, on the argument that the pre-registrations and negative
# results are the scientific record and should outlive any working tree. They are, and they still do:
# the record stays in git history and in the local tree. But a published repository is the wrong place
# to ship it -- a clone of a released tool should get the tool, not the internal argument about a
# module it cannot even build. Ignore the directory outright, with no exception to re-add it.
experiments/
docs/reports/
docs/review_followups.md
reports/


build_genotype_review/

